<div id="A1.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we added the results of Mistral v0.1 Instruct 7B and Mistral v0.3 Instruct 7B models on the QA2 and QA3 tasks of the BABILong benchmark, as shown in Table <a href="#A1.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ A.5 More Resutls on BABILong ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ LAWCAT: Efficient Distillation from Quadratic to Linear Attention with Convolution across Tokens for Long Context Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and Table <a href="#A1.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ A.5 More Resutls on BABILong ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ LAWCAT: Efficient Distillation from Quadratic to Linear Attention with Convolution across Tokens for Long Context Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> respectively.
As expected, applying LAWCAT to the models after instruction tuning will result in more stable performance, and the results of the Mistral models are consistent with those of the Llama model (although there are slight accuracy drops for the Mistral v0.1 7B Instruct LAWCAT variant model on QA3 when the context length is longer than 16k).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, we believe these results further validate the effectiveness of our LAWCAT on various model architectures and tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
